{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "מי אנכי", :translit "mi anokhi", :gloss "Who am I"} {:he "מי אנכי הביאותני עד הלום", :translit "mi anokhi hevi'otani ad halom", :gloss "Who am I that you have brought me until here"} {:he "אל תקרב הלום", :translit "al tikrav halom", :gloss "Do not come near here"} {:he "שלח נא ביד תשלח", :translit "shelach na b'yad tishlach", :gloss "Send now by the hand You will send"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["מי אנכי" "מי אנכי הביאותני עד הלום"]} {:kind :other, :note "Word הלום shared between II Samuel 7:18 and Exodus 3:11 links Moses' request to Davidic kingship"}], :frame {:text "In II Samuel 7:18, David says מי אנכי הביאותני עד הלום — 'Who am I that You have brought me until here?' — reflecting on God's promise of an eternal dynasty (II Samuel 7:16). The word הלום in Exodus 3:11, where Moses says מי אנכי, creates a verbal link between the two passages. The midrashic tradition reads Moses' protest as connected to his hope for an enduring royal line; when told אל תקרב הלום (Exodus 3:5), he understood הלום as a reference to kingship being withheld from his descendants (Exodus Rabbah 3:1–2). Moses' descendants are identified as Levites, not kings; the priesthood remained with his line through Aaron (Exodus 28:1), while kingship passed to David's line (II Samuel 7:16).", :sources ["II Samuel 7:16" "II Samuel 7:18" "Exodus 3:5" "Exodus 3:11" "Exodus 28:1" "Exodus Rabbah 3:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["מי אנכי" "מי אנכי הביאותני עד הלום"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "מי אנכי", :refs ["Exodus 3:11" "1 Samuel 18:18" "2 Samuel 7:18"], :n 3, :lens :exact} {:phrase "מי אנכי הביאותני עד הלום", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Word הלום shared between II Samuel 7:18 and Exodus 3:11 links Moses' request to Davidic kingship", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-